### What is the Flood Fill problem?

Flood Fill takes a 2D grid, a starting cell (row, col), and a new color. It repaints the starting cell and every cell connected to it through up, down, left, and right neighbors that shares the original color of the starting cell. Cells with a different color act as boundaries. This is exactly how the paint-bucket tool works in image editors.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class RiskZoneEscalator {
    public int[][] floodFill(int[][] grid, int sr, int sc,
                              int newColor) {
        int originalColor = grid[sr][sc];
        if (originalColor == newColor) return grid;  // critical guard
        dfs(grid, sr, sc, originalColor, newColor);
        return grid;
    }

    private void dfs(int[][] grid, int r, int c,
                      int original, int newColor) {
        if (r < 0 || r >= grid.length
                || c < 0 || c >= grid[0].length
                || grid[r][c] != original) {
            return;
        }
        grid[r][c] = newColor;  // repaint before recursing
        dfs(grid, r - 1, c, original, newColor);
        dfs(grid, r + 1, c, original, newColor);
        dfs(grid, r, c - 1, original, newColor);
        dfs(grid, r, c + 1, original, newColor);
    }
}
```

### Why is the early return guard essential?

If the new color is the same as the original color, every cell you repaint still matches the original, so the recursion never stops. The DFS version recurses forever and overflows the stack. The BFS version enqueues the same cells endlessly. The one-line guard `if (originalColor == newColor) return grid;` prevents both.

```java
// WITHOUT guard: infinite recursion when newColor == originalColor
// grid[r][c] = newColor; // cell still matches originalColor!
// dfs(grid, r-1, c, ...) // neighbor is the cell we just repainted!
// ... → StackOverflowError

// WITH guard: return immediately, no work needed
if (originalColor == newColor) return grid;
```

### How does the DFS approach work step by step?

At each cell, check bounds and color match. If valid, repaint the cell and recurse into all four neighbors. Repainting before recursing is what prevents revisiting: neighbors check the grid value and see the new color, which does not match the original, so they stop.

```text
Grid (original=1, newColor=2):
  1 1 0
  1 0 0
  0 0 0

Start: (0,0)
  (0,0)=1 → paint 2, recurse up, down, left, right
    up: out of bounds
    down: (1,0)=1 → paint 2, recurse
      down: (2,0)=0 → stop (not original)
      right: (1,1)=0 → stop
    right: (0,1)=1 → paint 2, recurse
      right: (0,2)=0 → stop
      down: (1,1)=0 → stop

Result:
  2 2 0
  2 0 0
  0 0 0
```

### How does the BFS approach work?

Enqueue the start cell and repaint it. For each dequeued cell, check its four neighbors. If a neighbor is in bounds and matches the original color, repaint it and enqueue it. Repainting at enqueue time ensures each cell is enqueued at most once.

```java
public int[][] floodFillBFS(int[][] grid, int sr, int sc,
                             int newColor) {
    int original = grid[sr][sc];
    if (original == newColor) return grid;
    java.util.Queue<int[]> queue = new java.util.LinkedList<>();
    queue.offer(new int[]{sr, sc});
    grid[sr][sc] = newColor;
    int[][] dirs = {{-1,0},{1,0},{0,-1},{0,1}};
    while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
        int[] cell = queue.poll();
        for (int[] d : dirs) {
            int nr = cell[0] + d[0], nc = cell[1] + d[1];
            if (nr >= 0 && nr < grid.length
                    && nc >= 0 && nc < grid[0].length
                    && grid[nr][nc] == original) {
                grid[nr][nc] = newColor;
                queue.offer(new int[]{nr, nc});
            }
        }
    }
    return grid;
}
```

### How do DFS and BFS compare for Flood Fill?

| Aspect | DFS | BFS |
|--------|-----|-----|
| Data structure | Call stack (implicit) | Queue (explicit) |
| Fill order | Deep first, then backtrack | Level by level (outward rings) |
| Stack overflow risk | Yes on large regions | No |
| Code length | Shorter (recursive) | Longer (queue loop) |
| Time | O(region size) | O(region size) |

### What are edge cases?

- Start cell already has the new color: return immediately (the critical guard).
- Single-cell grid: repaint just that cell.
- Entire grid is the same color: the fill covers every cell.
- Start cell has a unique color not shared by any neighbor: only the start cell is repainted.

```java
// Edge case: start cell is the only one with original color
// Grid: 1 0
//        0 0
// Start: (0,0), newColor = 2
// Result: 2 0
//          0 0  (only the start cell changed)
```

### What are common mistakes?

The first and most dangerous mistake is forgetting the early return guard when newColor == originalColor. The second mistake is marking cells at dequeue time in BFS, causing cells to be enqueued multiple times. The third mistake is checking diagonal neighbors, which the problem does not count.

```java
// WRONG: mark at dequeue time
int[] cell = queue.poll();
grid[cell[0]][cell[1]] = newColor;  // too late!

// CORRECT: mark at enqueue time
grid[nr][nc] = newColor;
queue.offer(new int[]{nr, nc});
```

### What about using a separate visited array?

If you cannot modify the grid, use a boolean visited array instead of repainting. The logic is the same, but you check `visited[r][c]` instead of `grid[r][c] != original`. After the traversal, paint all visited cells with the new color.

```java
boolean[][] visited = new boolean[rows][cols];
// Use visited to track instead of modifying grid during traversal
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

At the National Bank of India, the risk team maintained a geographic grid where each cell carried a risk code. When a fraud cluster was confirmed in one zone, the system escalated the entire connected block of zones sharing that code. Flood Fill did exactly that, and the early-return guard made it safe to re-trigger without looping, which mattered because the risk team sometimes clicked the same zone twice. The same pattern applies to image processing, region labeling, and minesweeper reveal logic.
